1c:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"They repeat each other's work across sessions."}]," A fresh session on the same\ncommit rebuilds what yesterday's session already built. With a shared cache that\nis a download."]}]
1d:["$","p",null,{"children":["The general form: ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"cache hit rate rises with the number of participants who\nshare ancestry, and agents generate a great deal of shared ancestry."}]," A cache\nthat is a modest optimization for one developer becomes the dominant factor when\na fleet of agents is hammering the same tree."]}]
1e:["$","$L1b",null,{"as":"h2","id":"determinism-is-the-other-half","children":"Determinism is the other half"}]
1f:["$","p",null,{"children":"Content-addressing gives agents something beyond speed, and it may matter more."}]
20:["$","p",null,{"children":"An agent debugging a failure needs the failure to be real. If a test fails\nbecause of a stale artifact, a leaked environment variable, or a machine that\ndiffers from the one that built the dependency, the agent will chase a\nphantom, and it will chase it with great energy, writing plausible fixes for a\nproblem that does not exist. Nondeterminism doesn't just slow an agent down; it\nsends it confidently in the wrong direction and burns tokens doing so."}]
21:["$","p",null,{"children":"Hermetic, content-addressed builds make \"same inputs, same outputs\" a property\nof the system rather than a hope. When an agent reruns a build and the result\nchanges, that change is now information."}]
22:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-amber-500/40 bg-amber-50/60 text-foreground dark:bg-amber-500/10","role":"alert","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M12 9v4m0 4h.01M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0Z"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"This cuts both ways"}],["$","p",null,{"children":["Remote execution requires hermetic actions, and a build that quietly depends\non the machine it runs on will fail remotely while working locally. That's\nreal work, and it's worth doing regardless: a build an agent can trust is a\nbuild a person can too. See\n",["$","$L33",null,{"href":"/remote-execution/toolchains-and-hermeticity","children":"Toolchains and hermeticity"}],"."]}]]}]]}]
23:["$","$L1b",null,{"as":"h2","id":"the-token-argument","children":"The token argument"}]
24:["$","p",null,{"children":"Wall clock is the visible cost. The token bill is the hidden one, and for\nteams running agents at scale it is frequently the larger."}]
25:["$","p",null,{"children":["Every iteration of the loop spends tokens: the agent reads build output,\nreasons about it, and produces the next edit. Slow builds don't just make the\nloop longer, they make it ",["$","em",null,{"children":"wider"}],": a long feedback delay pushes an agent toward\nbatching more speculative changes per iteration, which produces bigger diffs,\nnoisier failures, and more context to re-read when something breaks."]}]
26:["$","p",null,{"children":"Fast, trustworthy builds let an agent work in small verified increments. Small\nincrements mean less context re-read per iteration and fewer wasted iterations\nchasing failures that were never real."}]
27:["$","$L1b",null,{"as":"h2","id":"what-to-actually-put-in-place","children":"What to actually put in place"}]
28:["$","p",null,{"children":"In rough order of return:"}]
29:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"A shared cache both agents and humans use."}]," Not one per agent, not one for CI\nand another for developers. The value is in the sharing, and splitting it is the\nmost common way teams leave the win on the table.\n",["$","$L33",null,{"href":"/getting-started/shared-cache","children":"Shared cache"}]," is that deployment."]}]
2a:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Agents pointed at it from the start."}]," An agent that builds locally with no\nremote cache is generating exactly the redundant work this is designed to\nabsorb. This is one flag in the build configuration the agent already uses."]}]
2b:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Hermeticity treated as a bug class."}]," Every action that depends on unnamed\nstate is a source of failures your agent cannot debug and will misdiagnose."]}]
2c:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Remote execution once caching is steady."}]," Remote execution adds parallelism across a\nworker pool for the actions that genuinely have to run. It matters most when\nagents work in parallel, because that's when local cores become the ceiling."]}]
2d:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Enough cache retention to cover how agents actually work."}]," Eviction policy is\nusually sized for a linear history of commits. Agents branch, revert and\nrevisit far more than that, and a cache that has already evicted the tree an\nagent is returning to gives back the biggest single win on this page. Size for\nthe breadth of states in play, not just the depth of history."]}]
2e:["$","$L1b",null,{"as":"h2","id":"common-questions","children":"Common questions"}]
2f:["$","$L34",null,{"children":[["$","$L35",null,{"title":"Should each agent get its own cache to avoid interference?","id":"should-each-agent-get-its-own-cache-to-avoid-interference","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"No: that removes the main benefit. Cache entries are keyed by the hash of their\ninputs, so two agents cannot corrupt each other's results; the worst case is\nthat they store separate entries for genuinely different work. Isolation you\nmight want for other reasons is a network and access-control question, not a\ncache-partitioning one."}]}],["$","$L35",null,{"title":"Can a bad agent poison the cache for everyone?","id":"can-a-bad-agent-poison-the-cache-for-everyone","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Not through ordinary use. An entry is only reachable by the hash of the inputs\nthat produced it, so a bad edit produces a differently-keyed entry that nobody\nelse asks for. What ",["$","em",null,{"children":"can"}]," cause a bad shared entry is a non-hermetic action\nwhose output depends on something outside its declared inputs, which is the\nsame hazard for humans, and another reason hermeticity is the foundation. Write\naccess to the cache is a trust boundary; see\n",["$","$L33",null,{"href":"/how-to/tls-and-auth","children":"TLS and authentication"}],"."]}]}],["$","$L35",null,{"title":"Does this help with test flakiness?","id":"does-this-help-with-test-flakiness","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"Indirectly, and importantly. It doesn't fix a genuinely flaky test, but it\nremoves the large class of failures caused by stale or inconsistent build state,\nso the flakes that remain are real ones. For an agent, that distinction is the\ndifference between a debuggable signal and noise."}]}],["$","$L35",null,{"title":"Our agents work on many small repos, not one monorepo.","id":"our-agents-work-on-many-small-repos-not-one-monorepo","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"The benefit tracks shared ancestry, so it's weaker across unrelated repos and\nstill real within each one plus their common dependencies. If the repos share a\ntoolchain or a base image, those actions are shared cache entries even when the\nsource trees aren't."}]}],["$","$L35",null,{"title":"How do I point my agent at Forge?","id":"how-do-i-point-my-agent-at-forge","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["You don't configure the agent, you configure the build tool the agent invokes,\nand the agent picks it up because it runs the same commands you do. A remote\ncache flag in the checked-in build configuration is inherited by every agent and\nevery human automatically. ",["$","$L33",null,{"href":"/getting-started/quickstart","children":"Quickstart"}]," sets that\nup."]}]}]]}]
30:["$","a",null,{"href":"/getting-started/shared-cache","data-next-step":"next","className":"my-4 flex items-start gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 no-underline transition-colors border-brand/40 bg-brand-soft/30 hover:border-brand/70","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"18","height":"18","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-1 shrink-0 text-brand","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M5 12h14m-6-6 6 6-6 6"}]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"block font-mono text-[0.7rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-muted-foreground","children":"Next"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"block font-semibold text-foreground","children":"Shared cache"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 block text-sm text-muted-foreground","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"The deployment this page argues for: one cache, shared by everyone and\neverything that builds the tree."}]}]]}]]}]
